# Orrery — Screens, menus, and audio

This file defines the screens the game moves between, the two panels drawn
over a run, the menus, and the audio cues. The editor screen's own regions
and behavior are in `specs/editor.md`, the two modes and their select screens
in `specs/modes/campaign.md` and `specs/modes/extras.md`, and the input in
`specs/controls.md`. Every figure and every piece of screen copy below
carries the name this specification gives it.

## Presentation

Orrery fixes no palette, no font, no artwork, and no background. What it
fixes about appearance is stated as legibility, in `specs/field.md`,
`specs/parts.md`, and `specs/editor.md`. One requirement is this file's own:
every piece of text a screen shows is legible against whatever sits behind it
at the logical stage size `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`).

## Screens

`state.screen` names the screen the game is in: `title`, `howto`, `select`,
or `editor`. `state.mode` is `campaign` or `extras`, and decides which course
the `select` and `editor` screens serve.

### `title`

The main menu, and where the game opens.

| Element | Constant | Content |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | `TITLE_TEXT` | `ORRERY` |
| Tagline | `TAGLINE_TEXT` | `SET THE HEAVENS TURNING` |
| Menu | `TITLE_ITEMS` | `CAMPAIGN`, `EXTRAS`, `HOW TO PLAY`, in that order |

The menu's items are stacked under the title and tagline. The item at
`state.menuIndex` is highlighted and drawn distinctly, `menuIndex` is `0` on
arriving, `up` and `down` move the highlight and wrap at both ends, and
`back` does nothing.

`confirm` takes the highlighted item:

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `CAMPAIGN` | Sets `state.mode` to `campaign` and goes to `select`. |
| `EXTRAS` | Sets `state.mode` to `extras` and goes to `select`. |
| `HOW TO PLAY` | Goes to `howto`, page `0`. |

Progress, records, and per-challenge machines survive visits to the title:
choosing a mode again finds the session exactly as it was left.

### `howto`

How to play, told in a player's words rather than as rules of a system, over
`HOWTO_PAGES` (`5`) pages. `state.howtoPage` names the page shown, `0` on
arriving; `left` and `right` move it by one, stopping at `0` and
`HOWTO_PAGES - 1`, and the current page is indicated on screen. `back`
returns to `title` with `menuIndex` `0`. The pages, in order:

1. The sky: the hex field, motes and their classes, filaments,
   constellations, and that a challenge asks for products made from reagents.
2. The machine: rises and sets, arms and grippers, the wheel, track, and each
   sigil's engraving in one line apiece.
3. The tape: instructions, how every tape loops on one shared period, and
   how to write a tape, naming the keys.
4. Running: play, step, and speed, naming the keys; that motes collide; and
   that a fault stops the machine and where to read what went wrong.
5. Finishing: the target tally, the three metrics, and that lower is better.

### `select`

The list of the current mode's challenges. `specs/modes/campaign.md` and
`specs/modes/extras.md` define it for their modes; the two share one screen
and differ only in the list they show and the locking they apply.

### `editor`

The machine screen `specs/editor.md` defines, hosting both editing and the
run. The two panels below are drawn over it while a run is in the matching
status, without hiding the field.

## The solved panel

While `sim.status` is `complete`, a panel is drawn over the run showing
`SOLVED_TITLE_TEXT` (`CHALLENGE COMPLETE`), the finished run's three metrics,
the challenge's records with any new best marked, and a vertical menu built
from `SOLVED_ITEMS` (`NEXT CHALLENGE`, `KEEP TINKERING`, `BACK TO SELECT`, in
that order). `NEXT CHALLENGE` is offered only when the mode has a challenge
after this one; the menu is otherwise the remaining two items in the same
order. `state.menuIndex` is `0` on arriving, `up` and `down` move it with
wrapping, and `confirm` takes the item:

| Item | Does |
| --- | --- |
| `NEXT CHALLENGE` | Opens the next challenge of the mode in the editor. |
| `KEEP TINKERING` | Stops the run and returns to editing, machine intact. |
| `BACK TO SELECT` | Goes to `select`. |

`back` stops the run and returns to editing, machine intact, exactly as
`KEEP TINKERING` does. The finished machine stays visible behind the panel.

A challenge loaded directly through the surface of `specs/instrumentation.md`
belongs to no course, so its panel never offers `NEXT CHALLENGE`, and
`BACK TO SELECT` goes to the current `state.mode`'s select screen with every
progress figure and record untouched.

## The fault display

While `sim.status` is `faulted`, the field shows the machine frozen where the
fault caught it, the parts and motes at fault visibly marked, and a banner
names the fault by its `FAULTS` name from `specs/simulation.md` in words a
player can act on. The wording is yours; the fault kind and the marking are
not. `back` returns to editing.

## What advances on each screen

`state.simTime` accumulates the frame's delta time on every update, whatever
the screen, and input is read on every screen. Beyond that:

| Screen | What advances |
| --- | --- |
| `title`, `howto`, `select` | Nothing. |
| `editor` | The run, while `sim.status` is `running`, as `specs/simulation.md` defines. |

## Menu navigation

Menus and select lists are keyboard only, through the registered actions:
`up`, `down`, `left`, `right`, `confirm`, and `back`. The pointer operates
the editor alone. `mute` is read on every screen.

## Audio

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The audio layer is part of the runtime you write. Define and play exactly the
six cues in `CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{else}}
Audio plays through the engine's cue bus. Define and play exactly the six
cues in `CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:
{{/if}}

| Cue | Constant | Played when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `place` | `CUES.place` | A part is placed or moved. |
| `erase` | `CUES.erase` | A part is removed. |
| `start` | `CUES.start` | A run starts. |
| `halt` | `CUES.halt` | A run faults. |
| `constellation` | `CUES.constellation` | A set consumes one or more constellations. |
| `complete` | `CUES.complete` | The run completes. |

Each is a distinct short sound, played on the frame of its event and at most
once on that frame, however many of the event fired within it. The cues are
the same in both modes.

Muting and the first-interaction unlock belong to the runtime. The game binds
the `mute` action to the runtime's mute bit and toggles it from any screen,
then mirrors that bit into `state.muted` every frame. The game stays fully
playable with sound muted.

## Out of scope

- Network or online play.
- Touch and gamepad input. The editor is driven by the pointer and the
  keyboard.
- Persistence of progress, records, machines, or settings between sessions.
  Each session starts fresh.
